  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 110 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThe flow aloft begins to amplify Wednesday morning, as the \nlarge upper high moves east towards the Ohio Valley, while an \nupper trough, with multiple large upper waves passing through it\nbuilds into the central Rocky Mountain states. The first of \nseveral waves is forecast to pass well north of Iowa by all \navailable guidance Wednesday night into Thursday ridging \nbuilding into the area in its wake, resulting in a dry forecast \nthrough Friday, with the main impact being warmer temperatures \nand breezy conditions.\n\nThe upper trof then will reload for late week, continuing the \nstretch of southerly winds and warm temperatures Thursday through \nSaturday. The NBM continues to show a good potential for mid, and \npossibly upper 60s for each of these days! It will also be\nbreezy on Friday and Saturday with winds gusting to 20 to 25 MPH\nwith the higher gusts on Friday afternoon. That said, there \nwill be increased cloud cover for Saturday, as the front \napproaches, along with rain chances in the 30 to 60% range. The \nQPF from this system continues to show potential of less than \n0.25 in all areas, as it is expected to be starved for moisture,\nwith a ridge of high pressure from the lower Appalachians \nthrough Oklahoma cutting off any Gulf moisture. \n\nFollowing the warm breezy days late this week, will be a seasonal \ncold front arriving  Saturday night or early Sunday, with highs \nlimited to the 40s behind it. (which is far closer to normal than \nmid to upper 60s!).",
